{
  "dom_id": "step-q276764",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\u03a6(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X\u223cN(5,{6}^{2}),$ compute $P(X<2).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.2743\\,$ | $\\,0.2709\\,$ | $\\,0.2676\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.3085\\,$ | $\\,0.3050\\,$ | $\\,0.3015\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3446\\,$ | $\\,0.3409\\,$ | $\\,0.3372\\,$ |\n\n$P(X<2)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Given a normal random variable $X\u223cN(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X<x)=P(Z<\\frac{x-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z\u223cN(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =5$ and $\\sigma =6.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & =P(Z<\\frac{2-5}{6}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-0.5) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.5) \\\\  & =\u03a6(-0.5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/6d13179aa11fed4255ce0997c71063a6398fdbb9c6124a4048c9a29759317ab8.png)\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\u03a6(-0.5)=0.3085.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(X<2)=0.3085.\n$$",
